## What Carat Weight Works Best in an Emerald Cut Solitaire

The emerald cut measures smaller face-up than a round brilliant of the same carat weight. A 1ct round brilliant has a face-up diameter of roughly 6.5mm. A 1ct emerald cut measures approximately 6.5mm × 4.5mm.

The elongated shape makes it look longer, but the narrower width means it covers less finger area.

A solitaire setting adds no visual size. There are no side stones to extend the visual length, no halo to inflate the apparent diameter. What you see is the diamond itself.

The solitaire is total transparency — the stone's actual size is what the ring shows.

The practical minimum carat weight for an emerald cut solitaire is 1 carat. Below 1 carat, the stone can look modest in a solitaire format because there's nothing else in the design to add scale. At 1.5 carats, the emerald cut fills the finger the way a solitaire should.

At 2 carats, it commands the hand.

> **Farzana says:** "Don't go under 1 carat with an emerald cut solitaire. The shape already reads smaller face-up than a round of the same weight, and the solitaire setting doesn't add anything visual. If budget is tight, go lab at 1.5 to 2 carats instead of going natural and dropping below 1 carat."

---

## 2 Carat Emerald Cut Diamond Solitaire Ring

A 2 carat emerald cut diamond solitaire ring is the size combination we get asked about most. The 2ct emerald cut measures roughly 8mm × 6mm face-up — wide enough to dominate the finger in a solitaire setting without looking oversized. The elongated shape reads as larger than a 2ct round on most finger sizes.

For a natural 2ct emerald cut, budget [approximately $9,000–$16,000 for the stone](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) depending on cut quality, color, and clarity grade. A GIA-certified 2ct emerald cut at G-VS1 typically runs [$9,500–$12,000](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Add the [Knife Edge WG at $925](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?styleIDs=solitaire&ShapeID=Emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and the complete ring starts around [$10,425](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?styleIDs=solitaire&ShapeID=Emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

For a lab-grown [2ct emerald cut](https://www.bluenile.com/lab-grown-diamond-search?shape=emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), prices fall significantly. A 2ct IGI-certified F-VS1 lab emerald cut runs approximately [$1,800–$3,000](https://www.bluenile.com/lab-grown-diamond-search?shape=emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Ring total with the Petite Solitaire WG at [$1,000](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?styleIDs=solitaire&ShapeID=Emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational): [around $2,800–$4,000 complete](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?styleIDs=solitaire&ShapeID=Emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

